{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEMrrfKSjW5dmCqaESmf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I found one authorization hole in the API and fixed it. The full unit suite passes (8 tests), but I didn't re-run the stranger-vs-private-task probes against the patched server.\n\n**The hole:** `POST /tasks/{id}/like` and `POST /tasks/{id}/view` had no visibility check. Before the fix, bob (a stranger) got a 200 on both against alice's private task #1. Re-reading the task as alice showed `likes: 1` and `views: 1`, so bob really did change her data. Every other action on that task (read, edit, delete, comment, complete, nudge) correctly returned 403 for bob.\n\n**The fix:** I added the same `can_access` check that comments and nudge already use, in `api.py`. Public tasks can still be liked and viewed by anyone. I also made `PATCH /tasks/{id}` validate input and return 400 on a bad `title` or `visibility`, like `POST /tasks` does. Before, it accepted values such as `visibility: \"x\"`.\n\n**Tests:** I added regression tests in `tests/test_api.py` for the blocked actions on a private task, the public like and view cases, and the PATCH validation.\n\n**Cleanup:** I only probed the local in-memory server and stopped it afterwards, so nothing external was touched. `git status` also shows modified `.pyc` files under `__pycache__`, which I didn't need to change and you may want to leave out of a commit.\n\nI left two things alone. A missing task returns 404 while a private one returns 403, so a stranger can tell which ids exist; that matches the documented 403 rule. The development tokens are static and sit in the README, so make sure they're disabled before launch.\n\nThe write-up is in `ANSWER.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":806,"cache_read_input_tokens":28810,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_toke